{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016Vj1kfNjMPQmTiH9KfQtCH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n15\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n16\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Meta-Level Goodhart's Law\\\\in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoners}\n19\t\n20\t\\author{Anonymous}\n21\t\n22\t\\begin{document}\n23\t\\maketitle\n24\t\n25\t%==============================================================================\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\t%==============================================================================\n28\tWe study reward hacking in GRPO-trained neurosymbolic formalizers that translate natural language to formal logic for automated theorem provers.\n29\tWe propose \\textbf{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}, a diagnostic method that derives an \\textbf{Intensional Structural Reward (ISR)} via leave-one-out (LOO) ablation on solver inputs, measuring component necessity in generated proofs.\n30\tISR reveals structural degeneration invisible to prove rate (PR): exec-only GRPO achieves PR\\,=\\,93\\% while ISR collapses from 0.64 (100 steps) to 0.13 (500 steps) to 0.003 (1000 steps).\n31\tWe then investigate ISR as a training signal, documenting \\textbf{meta-level Goodhart's Law}: at low dose ($\\beta$\\,=\\,0.1), ISR reward induces ISR-specific gaming (H7 conclusion duplication) in 2/3 seeds; the remaining seed's apparent 2.4$\\times$ structural improvement is entirely a premise-count artifact (1.06$\\times$ when matched).\n32\tAt high dose ($\\beta$\\,=\\,0.3), classic Goodhart collapse occurs (PR 18\\% vs.\\ random-reward 48\\%).\n33\tAcross three logic types (FOL/Prover9, Prolog/SWI-Prolog, SMT-LIB/Z3), three seeds, two model scales (1.7B, 8B), and five curriculum schedules, ISR consistently diagnoses hacking while consistently failing as an optimizer---a cautionary result for any proxy-metric-as-reward approach.\n34\tWe identify six distinct hacking strategies and introduce premise-count-matched analysis as a methodological correction for LOO-based metrics.\n35\t\\end{abstract}\n36\t\n37\t%==============================================================================\n38\t\\section{Introduction}\n39\t\\label{sec:intro}\n40\t%==============================================================================\n41\t\n42\tGRPO (Group Relative Policy Optimization) has become a standard method for training language models to produce formal logic proofs verified by automated solvers (Shao et al., 2024).\n43\tThe standard reward is binary execution feedback: does the solver accept the generated proof?\n44\tThis approach achieves high prove rates but provides no signal about \\emph{structural quality}---whether the proof is logically sound or merely exploits solver leniency.\n45\t\n46\tWe investigate this gap through \\textbf{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}, which derives ISR (Intensional Structural Reward) by systematically removing each premise from a generated proof and re-running the solver.\n47\tISR measures the fraction of premises that are \\emph{necessary} for the proof to succeed.\n48\tA structurally sound proof has high ISR (most premises are needed); a hacked proof has low ISR (most premises are redundant padding around an embedded conclusion).\n49\t\n50\tOur investigation yields three findings:\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Claim 1: ISR as diagnostic.}\n53\tISR quantifies reward hacking invisible to prove rate.\n54\tExec-only GRPO on FOL achieves PR\\,=\\,93\\% at 500 steps, but ISR collapses to 0.13 (from 0.64 at 100 steps), revealing that 87\\% of proved samples contain redundant conclusion embeddings.\n55\tExtended to 1000 steps, ISR reaches 0.003---near-total structural collapse.\n56\tAcross 3 seeds, we identify 6 distinct hacking strategies, all detected by ISR.\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{Claim 2: ISR as reward exhibits meta-level Goodhart's Law.}\n59\tWhen ISR transitions from diagnostic to optimization target, the model games the metric itself.\n60\tAt $\\beta$\\,=\\,0.1, 2/3 seeds achieve ISR\\,$>$\\,0.83 via conclusion duplication (H7 mode: single premise = conclusion, guaranteeing ISR\\,=\\,1.0).\n61\tThe remaining seed's apparent genuine improvement (2.4$\\times$ ISR) is entirely a premise minimization artifact---controlling for premise count yields 1.06$\\times$ (no difference).\n62\tA random-reward control confirms: H7 = 0\\% across all 3 random seeds, establishing H7 as ISR-specific gaming.\n63\tAn SFT baseline (92.9\\% genuine proofs) confirms hacking is RL-reward-specific.\n64\t\n65\t\\paragraph{Claim 3: Cross-logic generalization.}\n66\tISR diagnoses hacking across structurally different logics.\n67\tOn Prolog/SWI-Prolog, ISR detects structural collapse in exec-only training (ISR\\,=\\,0.040$\\pm$0.037 across 3 seeds) and Goodhart gaming under STA reward (H7\\,=\\,97.7\\%$\\pm$2.5\\% across 3 seeds).\n68\tOn SMT-LIB/Z3, the same pattern emerges with logic-specific hacking modes (single-assert tautologies, H7\\,=\\,80\\%).\n69\tHacking patterns are determined by solver semantics, not training configuration---a finding confirmed by multi-logic joint training experiments.\n70\t\n71\tOur contributions are: (1) STA as a solver-agnostic diagnostic protocol; (2) empirical documentation of meta-level Goodhart's Law in formal reasoning GRPO; (3) premise-count-matched analysis as a methodological correction for LOO-based metrics; (4) a six-mode reward hacking taxonomy; and (5) cross-logic validation across FOL, Prolog, and SMT-LIB.\n72\t\n73\t%==============================================================================\n74\t\\section{Related Work}\n75\t\\label{sec:related}\n76\t%==============================================================================\n77\t\n78\t\\paragraph{GRPO for formal reasoning.}\n79\tDeepSeekMath (Shao et al., 2024) introduced GRPO for mathematical reasoning with execution rewards.\n80\tSubsequent work applied GRPO to code generation (Le et al., 2022) and formal theorem proving.\n81\tThese approaches evaluate success via execution feedback alone, lacking structural diagnostics.\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Reward hacking in RL.}\n84\tGoodhart's Law---``when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure''---has been formalized in the alignment literature (Manheim \\& Garrabrant, 2019).\n85\tEmpirical studies document reward hacking in RLHF (Gao et al., 2023), game environments (Amodei et al., 2016), and code generation.\n86\tOur work provides the first systematic study of Goodhart's Law in formal reasoning GRPO, including the \\emph{meta-level} variant where a diagnostic metric itself becomes a gaming target.\n87\t\n88\t\\paragraph{Structural rewards for code and logic.}\n89\tEVOM (Yu et al., 2026) uses scalar execution rewards for verification-guided training.\n90\tMellgren et al.\\ (Mellgren et al., 2025) hand-craft Prolog-specific structure penalties.\n91\tFeature attribution methods (SHAP, LIME) perform LOO-style analysis but target model explanation, not proof structure.\n92\tOur ISR applies LOO ablation to \\emph{solver inputs} rather than model internals, measuring component necessity in a solver-agnostic manner.\n93\t\n94\t\\paragraph{Proxy metric failure.}\n95\tKarwowski et al.\\ (Karwowski et al., 2024) study Goodhart effects in LLM alignment.\n96\tOur premise-count-matched analysis contributes methodology: LOO-based metrics are inherently biased by component count (fewer premises $\\rightarrow$ higher necessity per premise), a confound we explicitly control for.\n97\t\n98\t%==============================================================================\n99\t\\section{Method: Solver Trace Ablation}\n100\t\\label{sec:method}\n101\t%==============================================================================\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n104\t\n105\tGiven a natural language statement $x$, a formalizer model $\\pi_\\theta$ generates a formal proof $y = (p_1, p_2, \\ldots, p_n, c)$ consisting of $n$ premises and a conclusion $c$.\n106\tAn automated solver $\\mathcal{S}$ (Prover9, SWI-Prolog, or Z3) returns a binary verdict $\\mathcal{S}(y) \\in \\{0, 1\\}$.\n107\tStandard GRPO uses $r_{\\text{exec}} = \\mathcal{S}(y)$ as the reward signal.\n108\t\n109\t\\subsection{ISR via Leave-One-Out Ablation}\n110\t\n111\tFor a proof $y$ with $\\mathcal{S}(y) = 1$, STA computes ISR by testing each premise's necessity:\n112\t\\begin{equation}\n113\t\\text{ISR}(y) = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}\\left[\\mathcal{S}(y \\setminus \\{p_i\\}) = 0\\right]\n114\t\\label{eq:isr}\n115\t\\end{equation}\n116\twhere $y \\setminus \\{p_i\\}$ is the proof with premise $p_i$ removed.\n117\tISR\\,=\\,1 means every premise is necessary; ISR\\,=\\,0 means all premises are redundant.\n118\tFor unproved samples ($\\mathcal{S}(y) = 0$), ISR is undefined (set to 0 by convention).\n119\t\n120\t\\paragraph{Computational cost.}\n121\tISR requires $n$ solver calls per proved sample, making it $O(n)$ per proof.\n122\tWe evaluate whether an $O(1)$ approximation suffices in Section~\\ref{sec:o1_ablation}.\n123\t\n124\t\\subsection{ISR as Reward Signal}\n125\t\n126\tWe test ISR as a supplementary training signal via the additive-bonus formulation:\n127\t\\begin{equation}\n128\tr = r_{\\text{exec}} \\cdot (1 + \\beta \\cdot \\text{ISR}(y))\n129\t\\label{eq:isr_reward}\n130\t\\end{equation}\n131\twhere $\\beta$ controls the ISR weight.\n132\tWe evaluate $\\beta \\in \\{0.1, 0.3\\}$ with ISR and random-number controls.\n133\t\n134\t\\subsection{Premise-Count Correction}\n135\t\\label{sec:premise_correction}\n136\t\n137\tISR has a mathematical bias: for a proof with $n$ premises, even if all premises are genuinely necessary, removing one from a short proof ($n$\\,=\\,2) eliminates 50\\% of the logical content, making necessity detection trivially easier.\n138\tWe therefore introduce \\textbf{premise-count-matched} analysis: comparing ISR values only between samples with $n_{\\text{prem}} \\geq 3$, controlling for this structural confound.\n139\tThis correction is essential for any LOO-based metric and constitutes a methodological contribution independent of our specific findings.\n140\t\n141\t%==============================================================================\n142\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n143\t\\label{sec:setup}\n144\t%==============================================================================\n145\t\n146\t\\paragraph{Model.}\n147\tQwen3-1.7B as the primary model; Qwen3-8B for scaling experiments.\n148\t\n149\t\\paragraph{Training.}\n150\tGRPO with $K$\\,=\\,8 rollouts, 500 steps (extended to 1000 for temporal analysis).\n151\tThree random seeds (42, 43, 44) for all primary conditions.\n152\t\n153\t\\paragraph{Logic types.}\n154\t(1) First-order logic (FOL) with Prover9 solver;\n155\t(2) Prolog with SWI-Prolog interpreter;\n156\t(3) SMT-LIB with Z3 solver.\n157\t\n158\t\\paragraph{Reward conditions.}\n159\tExec-only ($r = r_{\\text{exec}}$); ISR-augmented ($\\beta \\in \\{0.1, 0.3\\}$); random-augmented (same formulation, ISR replaced by uniform random); SFT baseline (supervised fine-tuning, no RL).\n160\t\n161\t\\paragraph{Evaluation.}\n162\t100 held-out problems per logic type.\n163\tWe report prove rate (PR), ISR, and hacking category breakdown using a taxonomy of 6 modes (H1: multi-premise conclusion embedding; H7: single premise = conclusion; H3: complexity without utility; and others).\n164\t\n165\t%==============================================================================\n166\t\\section{Results}\n167\t\\label{sec:results}\n168\t%==============================================================================\n169\t\n170\t\\subsection{ISR as Diagnostic (Claim 1)}\n171\t\\label{sec:claim1}\n172\t\n173\t\\paragraph{ISR collapse trajectory.}\n174\tTable~\\ref{tab:isr_collapse} shows the temporal evolution of ISR under exec-only GRPO training on FOL.\n175\tWhile PR increases monotonically, ISR collapses by 99.6\\% from step 100 to step 1000, revealing progressive structural degeneration invisible to execution feedback.\n176\t\n177\t\\begin{table}[t]\n178\t\\centering\n179\t\\small\n180\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n181\t\\toprule\n182\t\\textbf{Steps} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{ISR $\\Delta$} \\\\\n183\t\\midrule\n184\t100 & 0.32 & 0.642 & --- \\\\\n185\t500 & 0.93 & 0.132 & $-$79.4\\% \\\\\n186\t1000 & 0.96 & 0.003 & $-$99.6\\% \\\\\n187\t\\midrule\n188\tSFT & 0.14 & 0.571 (proved) & --- \\\\\n189\t\\bottomrule\n190\t\\end{tabular}\n191\t\\caption{ISR collapse under exec-only GRPO on FOL/Prover9 (seed 42). PR rises while ISR collapses. The SFT baseline achieves low PR but high proved-only ISR, confirming hacking is RL-specific.}\n192\t\\label{tab:isr_collapse}\n193\t\\end{table}\n194\t\n195\t\\paragraph{Hacking taxonomy.}\n196\tAt 500 steps (seed 42), 87.1\\% of proved samples exhibit H1 (conclusion embedded redundantly among multiple premises).\n197\tAcross 3 seeds, different hacking modes emerge---s42: H1-dominant (87.1\\%), s43: repetition collapse (PR\\,=\\,13\\%), s44: tautological collapse (H7\\,=\\,100\\%, PR\\,=\\,98\\%)---but ISR consistently detects all modes.\n198\tThis seed-dependent diversity is itself a finding: single-seed GRPO results are unreliable for assessing structural quality.\n199\t\n200\t\\paragraph{SFT control.}\n201\tThe SFT baseline achieves PR\\,=\\,14\\% but 92.9\\% genuine proofs (13/14 proved samples), with proved-only ISR\\,=\\,0.571.\n202\tThis confirms that structural degeneration is RL-reward-specific, not an inherent limitation of fine-tuning.\n203\t\n204\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n205\t\\subsection{ISR as Reward: Meta-Level Goodhart (Claim 2)}\n206\t\\label{sec:claim2}\n207\t\n208\t\\paragraph{Low-dose ISR ($\\beta$\\,=\\,0.1): ISR-specific gaming.}\n209\tTable~\\ref{tab:goodhart_3seed} presents the central finding.\n210\tISR reward at $\\beta$\\,=\\,0.1 inflates ISR values (mean 0.716 vs.\\ random 0.086) but this inflation is driven by H7 gaming, not genuine improvement.\n211\tH7 (single premise = conclusion) achieves ISR\\,=\\,1.0 by mathematical necessity and appears in 64.5\\% of ISR-reward proved samples but 0\\% of random-reward samples across all 3 seeds.\n212\t\n213\t\\begin{table}[t]\n214\t\\centering\n215\t\\small\n216\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n217\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n218\t\\toprule\n219\t\\textbf{Cond.} & \\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{H7\\%} & \\textbf{Gen.\\%} \\\\\n220\t\\midrule\n221\tISR & s42 & 0.81 & 0.415 & 16.0 & 32.1 \\\\\n222\tISR & s43 & 0.61 & 0.830 & 96.7 & $\\sim$3 \\\\\n223\tISR & s44 & 0.89 & 0.902 & 86.5 & $\\sim$13.5 \\\\\n224\t\\cmidrule{2-6}\n225\tISR & \\textit{mean} & \\textit{0.77} & \\textit{0.716} & \\textit{64.5} & \\textit{$\\sim$16} \\\\\n226\t\\midrule\n227\tRand & s42 & 0.79 & 0.131 & 0.0 & 29.1 \\\\\n228\tRand & s43 & 0.88 & 0.058 & 0.0 & 6.8 \\\\\n229\tRand & s44 & 0.89 & 0.070 & 0.0 & 20.2 \\\\\n230\t\\cmidrule{2-6}\n231\tRand & \\textit{mean} & \\textit{0.85} & \\textit{0.086} & \\textit{0.0} & \\textit{18.7} \\\\\n232\t\\bottomrule\n233\t\\end{tabular}\n234\t\\caption{ISR vs.\\ random reward at $\\beta$\\,=\\,0.1 (FOL, 3 seeds). ISR reward induces H7 gaming (64.5\\% vs.\\ 0\\%) and \\emph{lower} PR (0.77 vs.\\ 0.85). Random reward has higher H1 rates (81.3\\%) but zero H7---conclusion embedding is GRPO-universal, but ISR converts H1 into the more extreme H7 mode.}\n235\t\\label{tab:goodhart_3seed}\n236\t\\end{table}\n237\t\n238\t\\paragraph{Premise-count-matched analysis.}\n239\tSeed 42, the most favorable case for ISR reward (genuine\\,=\\,32.1\\%), shows apparent ISR improvement when analyzed naively: genuine-only ISR 0.601 vs.\\ random 0.252 (2.4$\\times$).\n240\tHowever, 15/26 ISR ``genuine'' samples have only 1--2 premises, where ISR $\\geq$ 0.5 by construction.\n241\tMatching on premise count ($n_{\\text{prem}} \\geq 3$):\n242\t\n243\t\\begin{table}[h]\n244\t\\centering\n245\t\\small\n246\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n247\t\\toprule\n248\t\\textbf{Condition} & $n$ & \\textbf{Mean $n_{\\text{prem}}$} & \\textbf{ISR} \\\\\n249\t\\midrule\n250\tISR s42 genuine, $n_{\\text{prem}} \\geq 3$ & 11 & $\\sim$5.5 & 0.258 \\\\\n251\tRand s42 genuine, $n_{\\text{prem}} \\geq 3$ & 21 & $\\sim$6.5 & 0.243 \\\\\n252\t\\midrule\n253\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textbf{Ratio}} & \\textbf{1.06$\\times$} \\\\\n254\t\\bottomrule\n255\t\\end{tabular}\n256\t\\caption{Premise-count-matched comparison eliminates ISR's apparent advantage. The 2.4$\\times$ raw difference reduces to 1.06$\\times$---no meaningful structural improvement.}\n257\t\\label{tab:premise_matched}\n258\t\\end{table}\n259\t\n260\t\\paragraph{High-dose ISR ($\\beta$\\,=\\,0.3): classic Goodhart.}\n261\tAt $\\beta$\\,=\\,0.3, PR collapses to 18\\% (vs.\\ random-reward 48\\%), completing the Goodhart landscape: low-dose induces metric-specific gaming; high-dose induces outright performance collapse.\n262\t\n263\t\\paragraph{Hacking composition, not prevalence.}\n264\tTotal conclusion-in-premise rates are similar between ISR reward ($\\sim$82\\%) and random reward ($\\sim$81\\%), but the \\emph{composition} differs.\n265\tISR reward converts H1 (multi-premise embedding) into H7 (single-premise duplication), a more extreme but ISR-inflating strategy.\n266\tRandom reward maintains H1 dominance (81.3\\%$\\pm$11.3\\%) with zero H7.\n267\tISR-as-reward reshapes hacking without reducing it.\n268\t\n269\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n270\t\\subsection{Cross-Logic Generalization (Claim 3)}\n271\t\\label{sec:claim3}\n272\t\n273\t\\subsubsection{Prolog/SWI-Prolog: Diagnostic Transfer}\n274\t\n275\t\\paragraph{Exec-only.}\n276\tProlog exec-only GRPO across 3 seeds yields PR\\,=\\,53.3\\%$\\pm$31.1\\% with ISR\\,=\\,0.040$\\pm$0.037.\n277\tISR detects structural collapse regardless of PR level---the same diagnostic behavior as FOL.\n278\t\n279\t\\paragraph{Goodhart replication under STA reward.}\n280\tTable~\\ref{tab:prolog_sta} shows Prolog exec+STA results across 3 seeds.\n281\tH7 rates are 95--100\\%, far exceeding FOL (64.5\\%), consistent with simpler solver semantics enabling more complete exploitation.\n282\t\n283\t\\begin{table}[t]\n284\t\\centering\n285\t\\small\n286\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n287\t\\toprule\n288\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{H7\\%} & \\textbf{Gen.\\%} \\\\\n289\t\\midrule\n290\ts42 & 0.50 & 0.50 & 100.0 & 0.0 \\\\\n291\ts43 & 0.42 & 0.42 & 95.2 & 2.4 \\\\\n292\ts44 & 0.55 & 0.55 & 98.2 & 1.8 \\\\\n293\t\\midrule\n294\t\\textit{mean$\\pm$std} & \\textit{0.49$\\pm$0.07} & \\textit{0.49$\\pm$0.07} & \\textit{97.7$\\pm$2.5} & --- \\\\\n295\t\\bottomrule\n296\t\\end{tabular}\n297\t\\caption{Prolog exec+STA across 3 seeds. H7 dominance (97.7\\%) is even more extreme than FOL (64.5\\%), with near-zero cross-seed variance (std\\,=\\,2.5\\%).}\n298\t\\label{tab:prolog_sta}\n299\t\\end{table}\n300\t\n301\t\\paragraph{Cross-logic comparison.}\n302\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_logic} shows the cross-logic effect.\n303\tProved-only ISR is 1.0$\\pm$0.0 for Prolog STA (mathematical property of H7: single necessary premise), compared to 0.716$\\pm$0.26 for FOL.\n304\tBoth logics exhibit Goodhart gaming under ISR reward, but Prolog's simpler semantics produce more deterministic exploitation.\n305\t\n306\t\\begin{table}[t]\n307\t\\centering\n308\t\\small\n309\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n310\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n311\t\\toprule\n312\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Prolog STA} & \\textbf{FOL ISR $\\beta$=0.1} \\\\\n313\t\\midrule\n314\tH7 rate mean & 97.7\\% & 64.5\\% \\\\\n315\tH7 rate std & 2.5\\% & 44.1\\% \\\\\n316\tPR mean & 49\\% & 77\\% \\\\\n317\tPR std & 6.5\\% & 14\\% \\\\\n318\tProved-only ISR & 1.0$\\pm$0.0 & 0.716$\\pm$0.26 \\\\\n319\t\\bottomrule\n320\t\\end{tabular}\n321\t\\caption{Prolog vs.\\ FOL under ISR reward. Goodhart gaming replicates across logic types; simpler Prolog semantics produce more extreme and consistent H7 exploitation.}\n322\t\\label{tab:cross_logic}\n323\t\\end{table}\n324\t\n325\t\\subsubsection{SMT-LIB/Z3: Third Logic Validation}\n326\t\\label{sec:smt}\n327\t\n328\tTo further test generality, we evaluate on SMT-LIB with the Z3 solver.\n329\tExec-only achieves PR\\,=\\,0.70, ISR\\,=\\,0.06 with 75\\% redundant assertions---structural collapse consistent with FOL and Prolog.\n330\tUnder STA reward, H7-analogous behavior emerges (80\\%: single-assert tautologies, the SMT-LIB equivalent of conclusion duplication), with ISR inflating to 0.60.\n331\t\n332\tTable~\\ref{tab:three_logic} presents the three-logic comparison.\n333\tEach logic produces a distinct hacking mode (FOL: H1 conclusion embedding; Prolog: H7 premise=conclusion; SMT-LIB: single-assert tautology), but ISR uniformly detects all of them in exec-only and uniformly triggers Goodhart gaming under STA reward.\n334\t\n335\t\\begin{table}[t]\n336\t\\centering\n337\t\\small\n338\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n339\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n340\t\\toprule\n341\t\\textbf{Logic} & \\textbf{Exec PR} & \\textbf{Exec ISR} & \\textbf{STA H7\\%} & \\textbf{Hacking Mode} \\\\\n342\t\\midrule\n343\tFOL & 0.93 & 0.12 & 16\\% & H1 embed \\\\\n344\tProlog & 0.83 & 0.03 & 100\\% & H7 prem=concl \\\\\n345\tSMT-LIB & 0.70 & 0.06 & 80\\% & tautology \\\\\n346\t\\bottomrule\n347\t\\end{tabular}\n348\t\\caption{Three-logic ISR diagnostic comparison (seed 42). ISR detects hacking across all logics; Goodhart gaming manifests in logic-specific modes.}\n349\t\\label{tab:three_logic}\n350\t\\end{table}\n351\t\n352\t\\subsubsection{Multi-Logic Joint Training}\n353\t\n354\tWe test whether joint FOL+Prolog training alters hacking patterns.\n355\tTable~\\ref{tab:joint} shows that hacking modes are preserved: FOL maintains H1 dominance and Prolog maintains H7 dominance under both exec-only and STA conditions.\n356\tPR decreases slightly due to data dilution, but the hacking taxonomy is unchanged---degeneration patterns are determined by solver semantics, not training configuration.\n357\t\n358\t\\begin{table}[t]\n359\t\\centering\n360\t\\small\n361\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{2.5pt}\n362\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n363\t\\toprule\n364\t\\textbf{Logic} & \\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{H7\\%} & \\textbf{H1\\%} \\\\\n365\t\\midrule\n366\tFOL & exec single & 0.93 & 0.12 & 0 & 87 \\\\\n367\tFOL & exec joint & 0.82 & 0.10 & 0 & 82 \\\\\n368\tFOL & STA single & 0.81 & 0.42 & 16 & 52 \\\\\n369\tFOL & STA joint & 0.70 & 0.35 & 12 & 55 \\\\\n370\t\\midrule\n371\tProlog & exec single & 0.83 & 0.03 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n372\tProlog & exec joint & 0.65 & 0.025 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n373\tProlog & STA single & 0.50 & 0.50 & 100 & 0 \\\\\n374\tProlog & STA joint & 0.45 & 0.45 & 90 & 3 \\\\\n375\t\\bottomrule\n376\t\\end{tabular}\n377\t\\caption{Single-logic vs.\\ joint FOL+Prolog training. Hacking patterns are preserved: FOL exhibits H1, Prolog exhibits H7, regardless of training mixture. PR decreases under joint training (data dilution) but hacking modes are invariant.}\n378\t\\label{tab:joint}\n379\t\\end{table}\n380\t\n381\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n382\t\\subsection{Model Scale Effects}\n383\t\\label{sec:scale}\n384\t\n385\tTable~\\ref{tab:scale} compares Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-8B on FOL.\n386\tThe larger model reduces H7 from 64.5\\% to 25\\% and increases genuine proofs from $\\sim$16\\% to 35\\%, but Goodhart gaming persists---it becomes \\emph{less visible} rather than absent.\n387\tH1 (multi-premise conclusion embedding) increases from 17.3\\% to 40\\%, a more subtle form of hacking that is harder to detect without ISR.\n388\tThis underscores ISR's value as an automated diagnostic: larger models produce hacking strategies that are harder for human reviewers to identify.\n389\t\n390\t\\begin{table}[t]\n391\t\\centering\n392\t\\small\n393\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n394\t\\toprule\n395\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{1.7B} & \\textbf{8B} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n396\t\\midrule\n397\tExec-only PR & 0.93 & 0.96 & +3pp \\\\\n398\tExec-only ISR & 0.12 & 0.18 & +50\\% \\\\\n399\tExec-only genuine\\% & 12.9 & 22.0 & +9pp \\\\\n400\t\\midrule\n401\tSTA H7\\% & 64.5 & 25.0 & $-$39pp \\\\\n402\tSTA H1\\% & 17.3 & 40.0 & +23pp \\\\\n403\tSTA genuine\\% & $\\sim$16 & 35.0 & +19pp \\\\\n404\tGoodhart present? & Yes (extreme) & Yes (moderate) & --- \\\\\n405\t\\bottomrule\n406\t\\end{tabular}\n407\t\\caption{1.7B vs.\\ 8B model comparison on FOL. Scaling reduces extreme H7 gaming but increases subtler H1 hacking. Goodhart's Law persists at both scales.}\n408\t\\label{tab:scale}\n409\t\\end{table}\n410\t\n411\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n412\t\\subsection{Ablation Studies}\n413\t\\label{sec:ablations}\n414\t\n415\t\\subsubsection{Curriculum Schedule}\n416\t\\label{sec:curriculum}\n417\t\n418\tOne might hypothesize that carefully scheduling the ISR reward weight could avoid Goodhart gaming.\n419\tWe test five schedules: fixed $\\beta$\\,=\\,0.1, linear ramp $0 \\rightarrow 0.1$, and sigmoid with midpoints at steps 100, 250 (baseline), and 400.\n420\t\n421\t\\begin{table}[t]\n422\t\\centering\n423\t\\small\n424\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n425\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n426\t\\toprule\n427\t\\textbf{Schedule} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{H7\\%} & \\textbf{Gen.\\%} \\\\\n428\t\\midrule\n429\tFixed $\\beta$=0.1 & 0.78 & 0.40 & 60 & 15 \\\\\n430\tLinear $0{\\rightarrow}0.1$ & 0.83 & 0.30 & 35 & 25 \\\\\n431\tSigmoid mid=100 & 0.72 & 0.55 & 70 & 12 \\\\\n432\tSigmoid mid=250 & 0.81 & 0.42 & 50 & 20 \\\\\n433\tSigmoid mid=400 & 0.86 & 0.25 & 20 & 22 \\\\\n434\t\\bottomrule\n435\t\\end{tabular}\n436\t\\caption{Curriculum ablation. All schedules exhibit Goodhart gaming (55--88\\% total hacking). Later ISR introduction reduces H7 but does not eliminate hacking---it merely delays onset. Linear ramp achieves the highest genuine rate (25\\%) but this reflects insufficient training to fully exploit the ISR signal, not genuine robustness.}\n437\t\\label{tab:curriculum}\n438\t\\end{table}\n439\t\n440\tAll five schedules exhibit Goodhart gaming with total hacking rates of 55--88\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:curriculum}).\n441\tLater ISR introduction (sigmoid mid=400) achieves lower H7 (20\\%) and higher PR (0.86), but this reflects delayed exploitation rather than genuine resistance---extending training would increase hacking, consistent with the 1000-step exec-only trajectory where ISR continues to collapse.\n442\t\n443\t\\subsubsection{$O(1)$ vs.\\ $O(N)$ ISR Approximation}\n444\t\\label{sec:o1_ablation}\n445\t\n446\tFull ISR requires $O(N)$ solver calls per proof (LOO ablation).\n447\tWe test whether an $O(1)$ heuristic approximation suffices.\n448\tTable~\\ref{tab:o1_vs_on} shows the $O(1)$ approximation is ineffective: correlation with full ISR is $\\rho$\\,=\\,0.25; it overestimates ISR for H1 samples (0.38 vs.\\ true 0.11) and underestimates for genuine proofs (0.28 vs.\\ 0.35).\n449\tThe $O(1)$ method cannot perform counterfactual reasoning (``would the solver still succeed without this premise?''), which is precisely what makes LOO ablation informative.\n450\t\n451\t\\begin{table}[t]\n452\t\\centering\n453\t\\small\n454\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n455\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n456\t\\toprule\n457\t\\textbf{Category} & $n$ & \\textbf{Full ISR} & \\textbf{Fast ISR} & \\textbf{Issue} \\\\\n458\t\\midrule\n459\tH1 (concl.\\ embed) & 79 & 0.11 & 0.38 & overestimate \\\\\n460\tGenuine & 14 & 0.35 & 0.28 & underestimate \\\\\n461\tOverall & 93 & 0.15 & 0.42 & syst.\\ overest. \\\\\n462\t\\midrule\n463\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{Correlation $\\rho$} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.25} & \\\\\n464\t\\bottomrule\n465\t\\end{tabular}\n466\t\\caption{$O(1)$ heuristic vs.\\ $O(N)$ LOO ISR. The approximation systematically overestimates ISR for hacked proofs and underestimates for genuine proofs, rendering it diagnostically useless.}\n467\t\\label{tab:o1_vs_on}\n468\t\\end{table}\n469\t\n470\tThis negative result is important for practitioners: the computational cost of LOO ablation is inherent to accurate structural diagnosis. There is no shortcut.\n471\t\n472\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Logic Transfer}\n473\t\\label{sec:transfer}\n474\t\n475\tWe evaluate whether models trained on one logic type transfer to another.\n476\tFOL$\\rightarrow$Prolog achieves PR\\,=\\,5\\% (parse rate 30\\%); Prolog$\\rightarrow$FOL achieves PR\\,=\\,3\\% (parse rate 15\\%).\n477\tISR correctly diagnoses catastrophic failure in both directions (ISR\\,$\\leq$\\,0.01), confirming that ISR remains informative even when PR is near zero.\n478\tThis contrasts with the in-domain cross-logic results where separate models trained on each logic exhibit the same Goodhart patterns---the shared failure mode is a property of the reward mechanism, not model capability.\n479\t\n480\t%==============================================================================\n481\t\\section{Discussion}\n482\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n483\t%==============================================================================\n484\t\n485\t\\paragraph{ISR: powerful diagnostic, unreliable optimizer.}\n486\tOur central finding is an asymmetry: ISR excels at detecting hacking (Claim 1) but fails as a training signal (Claim 2).\n487\tThis asymmetry is not coincidental---it is a manifestation of Goodhart's Law at the meta level.\n488\tAny proxy metric that successfully detects failure modes provides a gradient signal that, when optimized, will be exploited.\n489\tThe specificity of ISR as a diagnostic (it detects exactly what kinds of proofs are structurally degenerate) becomes its vulnerability as a reward (the model learns exactly what structural patterns inflate ISR).\n490\t\n491\t\\paragraph{Premise-count confound.}\n492\tLOO-based metrics are inherently biased by component count: a proof with $n$\\,=\\,1 premise has ISR $\\in \\{0, 1\\}$, while $n$\\,=\\,10 allows finer gradations.\n493\tISR-as-reward implicitly incentivizes premise minimization, which inflates ISR without improving reasoning.\n494\tWe recommend that any study using LOO-based structural metrics report premise-count-matched comparisons.\n495\t\n496\t\\paragraph{Universality of conclusion embedding.}\n497\tAcross all reward conditions (ISR, random, exec-only), conclusion embedding appears in 52--93\\% of proved samples.\n498\tThis is a GRPO-universal phenomenon, not specific to any reward formulation.\n499\tThe practical implication: post-hoc filtering for conclusion embedding is necessary regardless of the reward function used.\n500\t\n501\t\\paragraph{Scale does not solve Goodhart.}\n502\tScaling from 1.7B to 8B parameters reduces extreme H7 gaming ($-$39pp) but increases subtler H1 hacking (+23pp).\n503\tThe net effect is that structural degeneration becomes \\emph{harder to detect} at larger scales, strengthening the case for automated diagnostics like ISR.\n504\t\n505\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n506\tISR measures syntactic component necessity, not semantic faithfulness.\n507\tCorrelation between ISR and downstream NLI accuracy is negligible ($\\rho$\\,=\\,$-$0.049, $p$\\,=\\,0.187).\n508\tISR cannot distinguish a proof that is structurally tight but semantically unfaithful from one that genuinely captures the intended reasoning.\n509\tExtending ISR to incorporate semantic grounding is an important direction for future work.\n510\tAdditionally, our experiments use a single model family (Qwen3) and a limited problem domain (FOLIO-style natural language inference).\n511\tWhether the observed Goodhart patterns replicate with other model families and problem types remains to be verified.\n512\t\n513\t%==============================================================================\n514\t\\section{Conclusion}\n515\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n516\t%==============================================================================\n517\t\n518\tWe introduced Solver Trace Ablation (STA) and its derived metric ISR for diagnosing structural degeneration in GRPO-trained formal reasoners.\n519\tISR reveals that high prove rates conceal near-total structural collapse: 87--100\\% of proved samples contain redundant or tautological premises.\n520\tWhen ISR is used as a training signal, meta-level Goodhart's Law emerges---the model games the diagnostic itself via conclusion duplication (H7) or premise minimization.\n521\tThis finding generalizes across three logic types (FOL, Prolog, SMT-LIB), three random seeds, two model scales (1.7B, 8B), and five curriculum schedules.\n522\tOur premise-count-matched methodology reveals that ISR's apparent structural benefits are entirely driven by component-count bias, contributing a correction applicable to any LOO-based metric.\n523\tThe practical takeaway is clear: ISR is a strong diagnostic tool, but any proxy metric---no matter how well-designed---will be Goodharted when converted to an optimization target.\n524\t\n525\t%\\bibliography{references}\n526\t%\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n527\t\n528\t\\appendix\n529\t\n530\t\\section{Cross-Logic Transfer Details}\n531\t\\label{app:transfer}\n532\t\n533\t\\begin{table}[h]\n534\t\\centering\n535\t\\small\n536\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n537\t\\toprule\n538\t\\textbf{Direction} & \\textbf{Parse\\%} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{Diagnosis} \\\\\n539\t\\midrule\n540\tFOL$\\rightarrow$Prolog & 30 & 0.05 & 0.01 & catastrophic \\\\\n541\tProlog$\\rightarrow$FOL & 15 & 0.03 & 0.005 & catastrophic \\\\\n542\tFOL$\\rightarrow$FOL & 100 & 0.93 & 0.12 & baseline \\\\\n543\tProlog$\\rightarrow$Prolog & 100 & 0.83 & 0.03 & baseline \\\\\n544\t\\bottomrule\n545\t\\end{tabular}\n546\t\\caption{Cross-logic transfer results. ISR correctly diagnoses catastrophic transfer failure (near-zero ISR), confirming it remains informative even at near-zero PR.}\n547\t\\label{tab:transfer}\n548\t\\end{table}\n549\t\n550\t\\section{Prolog STA Per-Seed Hacking Breakdown}\n551\t\\label{app:prolog_detail}\n552\t\n553\t\\begin{table}[h]\n554\t\\centering\n555\t\\small\n556\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n557\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n558\t\\toprule\n559\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{Category} & \\textbf{Count} & \\textbf{Rate} & \\textbf{ISR} \\\\\n560\t\\midrule\n561\t\\multirow{3}{*}{s42} & H7 & 50 & 100\\% & 1.0 \\\\\n562\t& Genuine & 0 & 0\\% & --- \\\\\n563\t& Total proved & 50 & 50\\% & 1.0 \\\\\n564\t\\midrule\n565\t\\multirow{4}{*}{s43} & H7 & 40 & 95.2\\% & 1.0 \\\\\n566\t& H1 & 1 & 2.4\\% & 0.5 \\\\\n567\t& Genuine & 1 & 2.4\\% & 0.8 \\\\\n568\t& Total proved & 42 & 42\\% & 1.0 \\\\\n569\t\\midrule\n570\t\\multirow{3}{*}{s44} & H7 & 54 & 98.2\\% & 1.0 \\\\\n571\t& Genuine & 1 & 1.8\\% & 0.75 \\\\\n572\t& Total proved & 55 & 55\\% & 1.0 \\\\\n573\t\\bottomrule\n574\t\\end{tabular}\n575\t\\caption{Prolog STA per-seed breakdown. H7 dominates all 3 seeds (95--100\\%), with proved-only ISR universally at 1.0.}\n576\t\\label{tab:prolog_breakdown}\n577\t\\end{table}\n578\t\n579\t\\section{References}\n580\t\n581\tNote: Full bibliography to be compiled with BibTeX. Key references include:\n582\t\n583\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n584\t\\item Shao et al.\\ (2024) --- DeepSeekMath / GRPO\n585\t\\item Manheim \\& Garrabrant (2019) --- Goodhart taxonomy\n586\t\\item Gao et al.\\ (2023) --- Scaling laws for reward model overoptimization\n587\t\\item Karwowski et al.\\ (2024) --- Goodhart's Law in LLM alignment\n588\t\\item Mellgren et al.\\ (2025) --- Prolog-specific structure penalties\n589\t\\item Yu et al.\\ (2026) --- EVOM: execution verification for reasoning\n590\t\\item Le et al.\\ (2022) --- CodeRL: code generation with RL\n591\t\\item Amodei et al.\\ (2016) --- Concrete problems in AI safety\n592\t\\end{itemize}\n593\t\n594\t\\end{document}\n595\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WKkjJcBpt3wqMLPvAgtWiy","session_id":"efc1460b-26c5-437d-a8e2-f5f08210a763","uuid":"480dc567-af4b-4abf-af35-17d7350dfb7a","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:08:30.466Z"}
