{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017RGJ5jC91aMJm2QVUX5sRJ","type":"tool_result","content":"33:Across three logic types (FOL/Prover9, Prolog/SWI-Prolog, SMT-LIB/Z3), three seeds, two model scales (1.7B, 8B), and five curriculum schedules, ISR consistently diagnoses hacking while consistently failing as an optimizer---a cautionary result for any proxy-metric-as-reward approach.\n147:Qwen3-1.7B as the primary model; Qwen3-8B for scaling experiments.\n385:Table~\\ref{tab:scale} compares Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-8B on FOL.\n395:\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{1.7B} & \\textbf{8B} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n407:\\caption{1.7B vs.\\ 8B model comparison on FOL. Scaling reduces extreme H7 gaming but increases subtler H1 hacking. Goodhart's Law persists at both scales.}\n502:Scaling from 1.7B to 8B parameters reduces extreme H7 gaming ($-$39pp) but increases subtler H1 hacking (+23pp).\n521:This finding generalizes across three logic types (FOL, Prolog, SMT-LIB), three random seeds, two model scales (1.7B, 8B), and five curriculum schedules."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WKkjJcBpt3wqMLPvAgtWiy","session_id":"efc1460b-26c5-437d-a8e2-f5f08210a763","uuid":"cb1dcc92-15ba-4140-9813-6dd2d035f8b6","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:09:04.353Z"}
